This project includes material from third-party open source software, and one
block of device data that is not software at all. The second is first, because
it is the one that needs explaining.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Flydigi factory profiles

flydigi/factory_config.py holds one 840-byte factory profile per controller
model. These are device state -- the bytes a pad holds in flash when it leaves
the factory -- and they are here because a per-slot restore has to write one:
the firmware's own reset (command 175) resets all four slots and ignores the
slot it is given, so restoring a single profile means writing the factory bytes
into it.

The two blobs have different provenance and it is worth stating which is which.

  * The Apex 5's was read off the controller on the author's desk after a
    factory reset, with tools/gen-factory-config. It is a measurement of
    hardware, on the same footing as flydigi/ds5_usb.py's captured DualSense
    descriptors.

  * The Vader 5 Pro's was derived from Flydigi Space Station's bundled
    Configs/Controller/f5/default/default_mapping_130.dat, translated into wire
    form by tools/mapping_bean.py. No Vader 5 was available to read.

The project's rule is that nothing Flydigi-owned is committed, and everything
gitignored is reproducible with tools/fetch-configs. This is the one deliberate
exception, made because the alternative was a feature that could not work: the
protocol is fully documented here, the derived form is 840 bytes of device
configuration rather than any part of their program, and there is no way to
obtain a Vader 5's factory profile without either the file or the hardware. The
translator is committed alongside it, so the derivation can be re-run and
checked rather than taken on trust -- tools/gen-factory-config --check holds it
against the Apex 5 blob read from hardware.

Neither blob is claimed as this project's work.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
inputtino
https://github.com/games-on-whales/inputtino

flydigi/ds5.py follows src/uhid/include/uhid/ps5.hpp for the DualSense's input
and output report layouts.

flydigi/ds5_usb.py carries inputtino's PS5_PAIRING_INFO Bluetooth addresses as
placeholders. They are substituted for the real controller's by
tools/gen_ds5_usb.py, unconditionally, so no hardware identity is committed --
inputtino's are already public in their repository.

The descriptors and feature-report blobs in that file are otherwise read from a
real controller, not derived from inputtino. flydigi/ps5_data.py, which was, has
been removed.

